      "en_spectrum_title": "🎚 Build a bond: the character is a slider, not a switch",
      "en_why_17": "Why 1.7? ",
      "en_why_17_body": "Put ΔEN = 1.7 into Pauling's formula and you get 51% ionic character. The famous cutoff is just the halfway mark, picked for convenience. Nothing snaps at 1.7: Al–Cl (1.55) and C–F (1.43) sit right below it and behave like strongly polar covalent bonds, not salts.",
      "en_zone_ionic": "mostly ionic",
      "en_zone_nonpolar": "nonpolar",
      "en_zone_polar": "polar covalent",
      "kin_axis_x": "reaction coordinate →",
      "kin_axis_y": "potential energy (kJ/mol)",
      "kin_boltz": "Share of collisions with enough energy",
      "kin_bust": "Drag the catalyst slider as far as it goes. The hill collapses, the rate jumps by orders of magnitude, and the two ends of the curve do not budge. A catalyst changes how fast you get there, never where you end up, and never the equilibrium position.",
      "kin_bust_label": "⚠ The one to watch: ",
